    "updated_at": "2026-08-09T17:55:08Z",
    "body": "**Verified on 1.18.15 (Windows 11, PowerShell)** — same root cause.\n\nWith a bash allowlist of `{\"*\": \"deny\", \"git log *\": \"allow\", \"git diff *\": \"allow\", ...}`:\n- `git diff --no-index a b` → correctly denied/prompted\n- `git diff --no-index -- a b` → executes with **no permission evaluation at all** (no allow/deny log line)\n\nThe shell parser treats a standalone `--` (or `-`) as an argument separator; the scanned pattern set ends up empty and the permission check returns early. Net effect: the bash allowlist is advisory only, not a security boundary.\n\nRelated observation from the same environment: `external_directory` config values need a trailing wildcard — a bare path (e.g. `\"C:\\\\dir\"`) never matches the glob the tool asks (`<dir>\\\\*`) and silently falls back to `ask`; \"always allow\" approvals persisted in the local DB can mask this.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
